WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.780
Glycolysis has two distinct phases.

2
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.780
In the investment phase, two ATP are consumed to phosphorylate glucose.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.460
In the payoff phase, four ATP are produced.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.240
Two from phosphoglycerate kinase and two from pyruvate kinase.

5
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.620
Subtract the investment from the payoff and the net yield is two ATP per glucose,

6
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:31.200
plus two NADH. Aerobic respiration then extracts roughly 30 more, which is why

7
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.360
oxygen-dependent metabolism is so much more efficient.

8
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:39.243
The central problem fermentation solves is a redox bookkeeping problem.

9
00:00:39.743 --> 00:00:46.423
Glycolysis consumes NAD+, +, and produces NADH at the glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate

10
00:00:46.423 --> 00:00:50.383
step. But the cell has only a finite NAD+ -plus pool.

11
00:00:51.263 --> 00:00:56.623
Fermentation regenerates NAD+ -plus by passing electrons from NADH onto pyruvate,

12
00:00:57.063 --> 00:00:58.843
producing either lactate or ethanol.

13
00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:05.103
The two cycles are tightly coupled, consume NAD+ -plus here, regenerate it there, so

14
00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:06.723
that glycolysis can keep running.

15
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.793
Here's a useful metaphor.

16
00:01:09.573 --> 00:01:11.833
Think of a metabolic pathway as a pipe.

17
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:15.773
A reversible reaction is like a valve you can partially close.

18
00:01:16.693 --> 00:01:18.273
Pressure equilibrates on both sides.

19
00:01:18.793 --> 00:01:21.453
An irreversible reaction is a one-way valve.

20
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:24.053
Close it and flow stops completely.

21
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:29.353
Cells regulate metabolism at irreversible steps precisely because that's where

22
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:30.813
control actually works.

23
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:34.623
Turn a knob at a reversible step and flux barely changes.

24
00:01:35.283 --> 00:01:38.083
Block an irreversible step and the whole line shuts down.

25
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:46.007
Glycolysis is regulated at three enzymes, hexokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, and

26
00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:49.767
pyruvate kinase. Each responds to different energy signals.

27
00:01:50.707 --> 00:01:55.007
Hexokinase is inhibited by its own product, glucose-6 -phosphate.

28
00:01:55.707 --> 00:02:02.027
PFK-1 is the master throttle, activated by AMP and fructose-2.6-bisphosphate,

29
00:02:02.427 --> 00:02:04.647
inhibited by ATP and citrate.

30
00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:10.407
Pyruvate kinase is activated feed-forward by fructose-1.6-bisphosphate.

31
00:02:10.827 --> 00:02:15.347
Together, these three checkpoints match glycolytic flux to the cell's energy needs.

32
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.160
Think of phosphofructokinase 1 as a turnstile.

33
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.100
Glucose must pass through it at step 3, which means the cell can regulate exactly

34
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.520
how much carbon flows into glycolysis.

35
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.080
Fructose, by contrast, sneaks in downstream.

36
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.840
It bypasses the gate and enters as D-chap and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate.

37
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.040
The result is uncontrolled carbon flow straight to pyruvate, which is why excess

38
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.580
dietary fructose can overwhelm normal metabolic regulation.
